1. **A method for jointly designing a gas turbine engine and a nacelle assembly**, the method comprising:  
   defining an engine architecture comprising a fan, a first compressor downstream of the fan, a second compressor downstream of the first compressor, a combustor, and at least one turbine configured to drive the fan and the first and second compressors;  
   defining a nacelle architecture surrounding at least a portion of the gas turbine engine and forming a bypass flow passage with the gas turbine engine;  
   iteratively varying at least one engine design parameter and at least one nacelle design parameter based on a performance model coupling aerodynamic performance of the gas turbine engine with aerodynamic performance of the nacelle assembly; and  
   selecting a jointly optimized configuration of the gas turbine engine and the nacelle assembly in which:  
   an overall pressure ratio across the first and second compressors is at least 35;  
   a pressure ratio across the fan is less than 1.7; and  
   a bypass ratio of airflow through the bypass flow passage to airflow through a core flow passage of the gas turbine engine is at least 5.

## Exemplary Disclosure: Interactive Engine-and-Nacelle Design Process

### Technical Field

The present disclosure relates to computer-assisted design of aircraft propulsion systems and, more particularly, to an interactive process for jointly configuring a gas-turbine engine and an associated nacelle. The process may be applied to a turbofan propulsion system in which a turbine-driven fan supplies an airflow that is divided between a compressor flowpath and a bypass duct.

### Propulsion-System Configuration

An exemplary propulsion assembly includes a nacelle defining an inlet, a bypass duct, and an exhaust region. A gas-turbine engine is positioned within the nacelle and includes, in serial flow communication, a fan, a compressor section, a combustor, and a turbine section. The turbine section is mechanically coupled to the fan, either directly or through a reduction gearbox, and drives the fan during operation.

Air entering the nacelle inlet is accelerated by the fan. Downstream of the fan, a splitter separates the fan discharge airflow into:

1. a core airflow directed into the compressor section; and  
2. a bypass airflow directed through an annular bypass duct disposed between an engine core casing and an outer nacelle structure.

The compressor section may include one or more low-pressure, intermediate-pressure, or high-pressure compressors. The turbine section may similarly include one or more turbine stages arranged to drive the fan and compressor rotors through corresponding shafts. The bypass and core streams may be discharged separately or combined in a downstream mixer before passing through an exhaust nozzle.

### Interactive Design System

A computer-implemented design system provides a graphical workspace in which a user can create, modify, and evaluate a coupled engine-and-nacelle model. The system maintains a parametric representation of the propulsion assembly, including geometric, aerodynamic, thermodynamic, acoustic, structural, and installation-related parameters.

The graphical workspace may present:

- a longitudinal cross-sectional view of the engine and nacelle;
- a three-dimensional model of the propulsion assembly;
- editable station locations and flowpath boundaries;
- performance maps or operating envelopes;
- constraint and warning indicators; and
- predicted propulsion-system performance.

User-adjustable parameters may include:

- fan diameter, hub diameter, and pressure ratio;
- fan rotational speed and blade count;
- corrected airflow and tip Mach number;
- compressor pressure ratio and stage count;
- core and bypass mass-flow rates;
- bypass ratio;
- splitter position and leading-edge geometry;
- inlet length, highlight diameter, and contraction ratio;
- nacelle maximum diameter and thickness distribution;
- bypass-duct area distribution;
- core-cowl and fan-cowl contours;
- nozzle throat and exit areas;
- gearbox ratio;
- shaft speeds;
- turbine inlet temperature;
- engine mounting position;
- ground-clearance and airframe-interface limits; and
- acoustic-treatment length and placement.

### Coupled Design Process

In an exemplary process, the system initially receives a set of mission and installation requirements. These requirements may include aircraft speed, altitude, thrust demand, fuel-consumption target, noise limit, nacelle drag limit, available installation volume, and minimum clearance from an aircraft surface or the ground.

The system then generates an initial engine cycle and estimates the airflow required to produce the specified thrust. Based on the selected fan operating point, the system determines a fan annulus area and corresponding fan diameter. A flow-split model determines the proportion of fan discharge air delivered to the compressor section and the proportion delivered to the bypass duct.

The flow split may be represented as:

\[
\dot{m}_{fan}=\dot{m}_{core}+\dot{m}_{bypass}
\]

where \(\dot{m}_{fan}\) is the total fan airflow, \(\dot{m}_{core}\) is the airflow entering the compressor section, and \(\dot{m}_{bypass}\) is the airflow entering the bypass duct. The bypass ratio may be calculated as:

\[
BPR=\frac{\dot{m}_{bypass}}{\dot{m}_{core}}
\]

The system uses the calculated or user-selected flow split to establish the splitter geometry, compressor inlet area, and bypass-duct area. The nacelle inlet and downstream flowpaths are then sized to satisfy allowable velocity, Mach number, diffusion, and pressure-loss criteria at one or more operating conditions.

### Interactive Modification and Automatic Propagation

A modification to one design parameter is automatically propagated to dependent engine and nacelle parameters. For example, when the user increases fan diameter, the system may automatically:

- resize the inlet highlight and throat;
- alter the nacelle external contour;
- update the fan rotational-speed limit;
- recalculate total fan airflow;
- revise the core-to-bypass flow split;
- resize the splitter and bypass duct;
- update nozzle areas;
- recalculate nacelle wetted area and drag;
- check installation clearances; and
- update thrust, fuel consumption, weight, and noise estimates.

Similarly, increasing compressor pressure ratio may cause the system to update compressor stage loading, core diameter, turbine work requirements, cooling-flow demand, turbine stage count, core-cowl geometry, and exhaust conditions.

The user may drag a displayed nacelle or flowpath control point. In response, the system modifies the associated geometric parameter and executes one or more analysis modules. Updated results may be displayed substantially in real time or after completion of a higher-fidelity analysis.

### Analysis and Constraint Management

The interactive process may employ analyses having different fidelity levels. A rapid preliminary model may be used while a parameter is being adjusted, followed by a more detailed model after the parameter is accepted. Available analyses may include:

- zero- or one-dimensional engine-cycle analysis;
- streamline-curvature or throughflow analysis;
- compressor and turbine map matching;
- inlet and bypass-duct pressure-loss prediction;
- computational fluid-dynamics analysis;
- nacelle drag estimation;
- structural and rotor-dynamic assessment;
- weight and center-of-gravity estimation;
- thermal-management analysis; and
- acoustic prediction.

The system compares the current design with predetermined constraints. A constraint may be classified as a hard constraint, which prevents acceptance of a design, or a soft constraint, which produces a warning or objective-function penalty. Exemplary constraints include fan tip speed, compressor surge margin, duct Mach number, inlet distortion, turbine temperature, shaft torque, nacelle thickness, structural clearance, maximum diameter, and allowable noise.

A violation may be identified directly on the graphical model. For example, a bypass-duct region having an excessive local Mach number may be highlighted, and the system may recommend increasing the local duct area, modifying the core-cowl contour, reducing bypass flow, or selecting a different fan operating point.

### Optimization

The system may support manual, automated, or mixed-initiative optimization. In a mixed-initiative mode, the user identifies design variables and objectives, while the system generates candidate designs. An exemplary objective function may account for fuel consumption, installed thrust, nacelle drag, propulsion-system mass, noise, and installation volume:

\[
J=w_1 TSFC+w_2 D_{nacelle}+w_3 W_{propulsion}+w_4 N-w_5 T_{installed}
\]

where the \(w\) terms are user-selected weighting factors.

The optimization routine may vary fan pressure ratio, bypass ratio, compressor pressure ratio, fan diameter, nacelle contour, bypass-duct area distribution, and nozzle area. Candidate configurations may be displayed on a trade-space plot. Selection of a candidate causes the corresponding engine and nacelle geometry to be loaded into the graphical workspace for further modification.

### Exemplary Operating Sequence

In one example, the user specifies a cruise thrust and an installation envelope. The system selects an initial turbine-driven fan and calculates a total fan airflow. A selected bypass ratio establishes the core and bypass mass flows. The compressor section is sized for the core flow, while the bypass duct is sized for the bypass flow.

The user then reduces nacelle diameter to improve installation clearance. The system detects a reduction in bypass-duct flow area and predicts an increase in bypass Mach number and pressure loss. The system presents alternative corrective actions, such as reducing core-cowl diameter, increasing nacelle length, changing the fan operating point, or reducing bypass ratio. After the user selects an action, the system updates the engine cycle, duct geometry, nozzle sizing, and installed-performance prediction.

The process continues iteratively until the propulsion assembly satisfies the selected performance and installation requirements.

### Design Data and Output

The system may store each accepted configuration as a versioned design state containing geometry, boundary conditions, component maps, assumptions, analysis results, and constraint status. Different configurations may be compared, merged, or restored.

Final outputs may include:

- engine station and cycle data;
- three-dimensional engine and nacelle geometry;
- compressor, turbine, and fan operating points;
- core and bypass mass-flow schedules;
- inlet, duct, and nozzle area distributions;
- predicted installed thrust and fuel consumption;
- drag, weight, noise, and thermal estimates;
- constraint-compliance reports; and
- manufacturing or downstream analysis files.

Accordingly, the disclosed process enables the fan, compressor section, airflow splitter, bypass duct, and nacelle to be designed as an integrated propulsion system rather than as independently sized components.

**Proposed Patent Claim Language**

1. An aircraft propulsion system comprising:  
   a gas turbine engine; and  
   a nacelle surrounding at least a portion of the gas turbine engine, the gas turbine engine and the nacelle being cooperatively configured as an integrated propulsion system;

   the gas turbine engine including:  
   a fan configured to generate an airflow;  
   a core flowpath configured to receive a first portion of the airflow from the fan;  
   a bypass duct configured to receive a second portion of the airflow from the fan;  
   at least one compressor disposed in the core flowpath; and  
   a turbine operatively coupled to the fan and the at least one compressor and configured to drive the fan and the at least one compressor;

   wherein the gas turbine engine has:  
   an overall pressure ratio of about 35 or greater;  
   a fan pressure ratio of less than about 1.7; and  
   a bypass ratio of about 5 or greater.
